      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-27-bluesky-adds-an-algorithmic-opt-out-feature-for-those-who-do",
      "title": "Bluesky adds an ‘algorithmic opt-out’ feature for those who don’t want to go viral",
      "summary": "Sometimes people just want to post to their followers, Bluesky says.",
      "body": "1:15 PM PDT · August 27, 2026\nImage Credits:Matteo Della Torre/NurPhoto (opens in a new window) / Getty Images\nAfter adding support for longer videos just yesterday, open social network Bluesky on Thursday introduced a new algorithmic opt-out feature that allows users to stop their posts from appearing in the app’s main Discover feed.\nThat algorithmic feed can currently surface any post on Bluesky’s network, as posts on the network are public by default.\nTo be clear, this latest change isn’t a way to make posts private — Bluesky is still working on rolling out support for private data at the protocol level. Instead, the feature simply makes a user’s public posts less discoverable to people outside their existing personal network.\nThe company says it created the feature because not everyone using its social media site wants to go viral. Sometimes, people just want to post for their followers without having their words exposed to larger crowds.\nTo opt out of having posts shown in the Discover feed, users can toggle on a new option in the app’s Privacy and Security settings. The change can take up to an hour to fully take effect, the company says.\nIt’s also worth noting that Bluesky’s implementation of the feature extends beyond its own app.\nInstead of just being a setting that applies only within Bluesky, the preference is recorded at the account level. That means the choice travels with the user, even if they’re posting from another app that is powered by the same underlying protocol that Bluesky uses, AT Proto.\nHowever, while those other apps have access to this information, they still have to choose whether to respect it.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Sarah Perez",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T20:15:26.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/01/bluesky-GettyImages-2185144073.jpg?w=1024",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/27/bluesky-adds-an-algorithmic-opt-out-feature-for-those-who-dont-want-to-go-viral/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-27-extra-200-police-drafted-in-to-aid-force-investigating-wrong",
      "title": "Extra 200 police drafted in to aid force investigating wrong-way crash and fire deaths",
      "summary": "Cleveland Police confirms it has support from six local forces and the National Crime Agency.",
      "body": "An extra 200 police officers are being sent to support an investigation into a car crash and a house fire which left nine people dead, a council leader has said.Seven people, including two police officers, died in the crash at South Bank near Middlesbrough on Saturday, followed by a house fire in Grangetown on Wednesday that claimed the lives of a woman aged 34 and a seven-year-old girl.Alec Brown, leader of Redcar and Cleveland Council, said armed officers, dog units and \"community bobbies\" were among the additional personnel supporting local detectives.Cleveland Police said it was receiving support from neighbouring forces, but exact numbers were \"changing all the time\".It confirmed staff from the National Crime Agency, and the Northumbria, Durham, Humberside and North, South and West Yorkshire forces were supporting local officers.Earlier Prime Minister Andy Burnham said \"all of the support will be provided to get to the heart of what has happened\" over the past days.Earlier Brown told Radio 4's Today programme that residents deserved to \"feel safe in their own homes\" and he understood people from the area might feel \"left behind\".\"I'm from an estate, from the same ilk,\" he said. \"You live around people that may be suffering poverty or other social issues, it can be that uniform sort of consciousness that 'we're left behind'.\"But I can assure the people of Grangetown and South Bank they're as important to me as people from any other estate in the borough.\"Local Labour MPs Anna Turley, Luke Myer, Andy McDonald, Jonathan Brash and Chris McDonald issued a joint statement in which they welcomed the help of other police forces during the past week, but called for more long-term support to protect residents who had expressed \"safety concerns\" for \"far too long\". Myer said current national funding formulas for the police and fire service did not take into account the \"seriousness\" of the crime people in Teesside faced. \"We essentially have a metropolitan style of crime and also risk of fire but with a sort of rural-style funding,\" he said.\"This is why we need to see a real overhaul of the funding formula.\"Liberal Democrat leader Sir Ed Davey told the Press Association the events in and around Middlesbrough over the last week were \"deeply worrying\".Who were the seven killed in wrong-way crash?Two police officers among seven killed in wrong-way car crashPCs killed in A66 crash were heroes, say familiesFundraiser for A66 crash police officers tops £1mOn Saturday, PC Matthew Blades and PC Tom Clough died when a Volkswagen Passat, which was going the wrong way on the A66 at South Bank, hit their vehicle.The five young men in the Volkswagen also died and inquests into all seven deaths opened on Thursday.Police said 17 people, aged between 18 and 61, had been arrested across Teesside as part of an investigation into the lead-up to the crash. They have been held on a range of offences including possession of a firearm and being part of an organised crime group.Cleveland Police are also investigating another crash in which a vehicle hit the wall of a house in South Bank in the early hours of Thursday morning. The property had been empty at the time and the force said it had \"not yet been confirmed whether this incident is linked to wider organised crime\".On Wednesday, Natalie McDonald, 34, died along with her seven-year-old niece Valentina Foster in the fire in Grangetown.Natalie's mother, Lisa Kirton, described her as \"a beautiful soul, who would have done anything for anybody\".Valentina, who was known as Tina, was staying at the house for the summer holidays and had been \"dancing around the living room\" only the previous night. Lisa said Natalie's 15-year-old daughter and a friend had managed to escape after the friend \"kicked out a window\" upstairs.Natalie's brother, Josh Kirton, said she had not been involved with \"anything to do with gangs\" or the crash on the A66.\"That's what makes this so senseless,\" he said.\"There's some terrible people in the world and now my sister and Valentina have gone.\"The fire also left a 48-year-old woman in a critical condition in hospital, while another three people suffered non-life threatening injuries.Two men, aged 23 and 31, were arrested on suspicion of murder and remained in custody.Cleveland Police said it was \"keeping an open mind\" about the fire and said it could not \"rule out any links to wider organised crime at this time\".Follow BBC Tees on X,, external Facebook, external, Nextdoor and Instagram, external and listen to Radio Tees on BBC Sounds.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T20:14:57.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/3441/live/f48b6120-a204-11f1-b109-879e35c24276.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckgvnjpv1l0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-27-buried-in-meta-s-18b-settlement-is-a-legal-pass-on-kids-data",
      "title": "Buried in Meta’s $18B settlement is a legal pass on kids’ data",
      "summary": "Meta’s settlement with 29 states allows it to retain certain data from children under 13 to train and test age-detection models, highlighting a privacy trade-off built into the deal.",
      "body": "In addition to paying out up to $18 billion and adding child safety measures, Meta’s settlement agreement with attorneys general from 29 states includes an interesting provision: The states have agreed not to sue Meta under existing child safety laws over its retention and use of children’s data.\nThat permission is being granted for the limited purpose of training and testing Meta’s age-assurance model and includes guardrails, but it’s a curious policy decision to make in a case centered on child safety, and one that could be difficult to properly enforce.\nAs specified in the settlement agreement, Meta must develop, train, and begin testing a model designed to detect which users on Meta’s platforms are under the age of 13. This must be done within a year of the document’s effective date. (While the agreement doesn’t specify that the model has to be AI-based, Meta’s current age-detection tools are powered by AI technology.)\nUnder U.S. child safety law, COPPA (Children’s Online Privacy Protection Act) typically requires that websites and apps limit the collection and retention of children’s personal information. Meta’s settlement agreement says that Meta shouldn’t need to violate COPPA to train or implement its age-assurance models. However, the agreement also says that the state AGs have agreed “fully, finally, and forever” not to bring any past, present, or future COPPA claims — or claims under similar state laws — related to Meta’s use of children’s data.\nThe agreement makes clear that Meta can’t use data from users under age 13 for ad targeting, marketing, or algorithmic optimization.\nMeta’s request for legal protection, and the state AGs’ willingness to grant it, isn’t unreasonable, noted one legal source TechCrunch spoke to, who couldn’t comment publicly due to a conflict. However, they pointed out there’s a caveat: COPPA is a federal law primarily enforced by the FTC, not the states, so it’s unclear whether the FTC, which isn’t a party to this settlement, has separately agreed to the same compromise.\nIt can be difficult for companies to keep data technically and organizationally isolated from the rest of their systems. Yet Meta is being asked to do just that —  to isolate its understanding of children’s behavior signals and other data and use it solely for detecting and removing under-13 users. Fortunately, an independent auditor will be involved in monitoring Meta’s compliance with the settlement so we don’t only have to rely on Meta’s word.\nPolicing this limitation could be complicated. The data could hypothetically feed into other Meta systems over time, or could raise questions over whether the data, signals, or insights derived from it are being used elsewhere within the company. What’s not clear from the agreement is what data Meta will retain for training the model, how much behavioral information that may include, or how long it will retain the data. We also don’t know how these models will change in the future as Meta meets the settlement’s terms.\nBarring state AGs from raising COPPA or similar state-law claims over this use of children’s data in the future could complicate the legal avenues states can pursue if questions arise around how Meta is using the data.\nThat doesn’t prevent them from pursuing legal claims, notes Joshua Wurtzel, a partner at Schlam Stone & Dolan LLP. “If Meta uses the data outside those lines, the release and covenant not to sue don’t apply,” he said. But those legal disputes could still be complicated, since they’d hinge on whether Meta’s use of the data fell within the settlement’s terms.\nPeter Jackson, a Data & IP attorney at Greenberg Glusker LLP, agrees, saying the carve-out here could “disincentivize future enforcement actions.”\n“The Settlement Agreement’s age-assurance measures bear all the hallmarks of a heavy, and perhaps hasty, negotiation,” he says.\nThe decision also touches on a broader question that’s been coming up across the AI industry lately, especially as more AI agents are being developed to help consumers with various tasks. The systems often require significant access to users’ personal data to work well. Similarly, Meta may need deep insight into children’s use of social media in order to identify which accounts belong to young people.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nSarah has worked as a reporter for TechCrunch since August 2011. She joined the company after having previously spent over three years at ReadWriteWeb. Prior to her work as a reporter, Sarah worked in I.T. across a number of industries, including banking, retail and software.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Sarah by emailing sarahp@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at sarahperez.01 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
