      "id": "theverge-2026-08-27-google-tells-android-app-developers-to-cool-it-on-memory-use",
      "title": "Google tells Android app developers to cool it on memory use, or else",
      "summary": "The memory crisis just won’t quit. | Image: Alex Castro / The Verge\t\n\nGoogle will start policing memory-hungry Android apps as a direct response to the RAM crisis. Spotted by TechCrunch, the company yesterday published a memo addressing the Play Store's role in enforcing new memory-usage restrictions. The post emphasizes the importance of meeting new memory usage limits for apps, in order \"to help",
      "body": "Google will start policing memory-hungry Android apps as a direct response to the RAM crisis. Spotted by TechCrunch, the company yesterday published a memo addressing the Play Store’s role in enforcing new memory-usage restrictions. The post emphasizes the importance of meeting new memory usage limits for apps, in order “to help developers navigate industry-wide hardware constraints and Android’s broader memory limits,” it says. And failing to meet the new memory restrictions will come with consequences.\nThe memory limitations were first introduced with Android 17, appearing first on Pixel phones. It’s probably no coincidence that the base configurations of the Pixel 11 Pro and 11 Pro XL come with less RAM than last year’s models. As Android 17 rolls out to the wider ecosystem, Google warns developers that they’ll need to play nice with a much bigger range of devices: “Over the coming year, an increasing number of manufacturers will leverage the Android per-app memory limits across their portfolio of device RAM configurations from 4GB to 16GB+ devices.”\nGoogle has a pretty clear directive if apps misbehave: “If your app exceeds these limits, it will be slowed down and may be terminated.” But yesterday’s post hints at even bigger… ramifications (I’m so sorry): “Apps and games that do not meet these thresholds may see reduced app visibility and publishing capabilities on Google Play. Additional details will be provided later this year.” Google says it will start enforcing the memory limits in February 2027, so app makers have some time to figure it out, but dang. That’s potentially pretty serious.\nThe memory shortage has had a well-documented impact on hardware, and it seems that it’s time for software developers to feel the squeeze, too. And Google isn’t playing around — it seems to be putting some real teeth behind its new memory policies.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Android",
        "Google",
        "Mobile",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Allison Johnson",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T18:33:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2025/05/acastro_STK112_android_01.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/985679/google-play-android-17-memory-limit",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
