      "id": "bbc-2026-08-27-the-papers-britons-lost-in-nepal-flood-and-regal-had-landed",
      "title": "The Papers: 'Britons lost in Nepal flood' and 'Regal had landed'",
      "summary": "The devastating flash flood and mudslides along the Nepal and Tibet border dominates Thursday's papers.",
      "body": "The front page of the Times, external charts the destruction at the Nepal-Tibet border with images of people running for their lives as mud, water and debris engulf a building. The Daily Mail describes, external the disaster as an  \"apocalyptic mudslide\" that \"wiped out everything in its path\".  The paper says witnesses thought they were seeing explosions caused by a military invasion, as clouds of debris were thrown up into the air.The i Paper, external on Meta's decision to pay billions of dollars to settle claims - which it denies - that Facebook and Instagram harmed children. The paper notes the firm's agreement to make apps safer for young people will not apply to the UK.The Guardian's analysis, external calls the introduction of default protections for teens \"a major concession\", that \"sets a precedent others in the tech industry are likely to follow\".  \"Well that's told 'em\", quips the Sun's columnist, external, Colin Robertson, who points out the cost of the settlement represents just over one per cent of Meta's $1.4 trillion value.The Daily Telegraph says, external it understands that Germany is in talks to invest in Britain's nuclear deterrent, as part of efforts to reduce Europe's reliance on the US.  The paper says military planners in Berlin believe they could contribute air defences to key locations, such as the base in Faslane that houses submarines armed with the UK's Trident missiles.Andy Burnham has spoken the Financial Times, external in what it describes as his \"most pro-business interview since becoming prime minister\". He tells the FT he's \"acutely conscious of how hard the operating environment is for businesses\", but declines to say whether taxes on companies would rise in the upcoming Budget.The coverage of the outpouring of affection for the late queen of country, Dolly Parton, spills into a second day, with jourrnalists who were lucky enough to interview her setting out their memories.  In the Daily Express, external, Paul Sexton describes her as \"side-splittingly funny\" and \"bigger than country music itself\". The Daily Mirror's, external Tom Bryant recalls how she made him feel \"at the centre of her world, despite her enormous fame\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T04:31:10.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/080a/live/db2f0d20-a19c-11f1-b109-879e35c24276.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cx2zkek78j5o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-27-trial-date-set-for-alleged-9-11-mastermind-khalid-sheikh-moh",
      "title": "Trial date set for alleged 9/11 mastermind Khalid Sheikh Mohammed",
      "summary": "His plea deal to avoid a death-penalty trial was rejected by a federal appeals court last summer.",
      "body": "Alleged 9/11 terror attacks mastermind Khalid Sheikh Mohammed will stand trial in June 2028, more than a quarter of a century after what the US government has called \"the most egregious criminal act on American soil in modern history\".A military judge ruled the trial would start in two years because a 2027 date sought by prosecutors was too soon.Mohammed had reached a plea deal where he would admit guilt in exchange for avoiding a death-penalty trial, but a federal appeals court threw out the agreement last summer. Arrested in 2003, he has been held at Guantanamo Bay since September 2006.Mohammed has previously said he planned 2001's \"9/11 operation from A-to-Z\".US Air Force judge Lt Col Michael A Schrama ruled on Wednesday that the trial for Mohammed and three co-accused would start on 5 June, 2028. Alleged accomplices Walid bin Attash, Ali Abdul Aziz Ali and Mustafa al-Hawsawi were listed in the court document.The start date allowed for additional time to deal with matters of the court, such as resolving any disputes about what evidence can be presented, the judge wrote.Next month will mark 25 years since hijackers flew planes into the World Trade Center in New York City and the Pentagon in Virginia, while another flight they hijacked crashed in a field in Pennsylvania. About 3,000 people died in the attacks.Mohammed is accused of conceiving the idea of training pilots to fly commercial planes into buildings and taking those plans to Osama Bin Laden, leader of al-Qaeda.Mohammed, a US-educated engineer, was captured along with al-Hawsawi in Pakistan in March 2003.The rejected plea deal, which would have given Mohammed a life sentence without possibility of parole, was reached after two years of negotiation and signed by an official appointed by then-Defence Secretary Lloyd Austin. But Austin was reportedly caught by surprise, and immediately attempted to revoke it.The military prison in Guantanamo Bay opened six months after the attacks. About 15 prisoners are still detained there.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-27T02:50:26.000Z",
