      "id": "theverge-2026-08-26-elden-ring-on-the-switch-2-isn-8217-t-tarnished",
      "title": "Elden Ring on the Switch 2 isn&#8217;t tarnished",
      "summary": "FromSoftware is hoping to make a splash on the Switch 2 later this year when it launches The Duskbloods, a gothic competitive multiplayer game that's unlike anything the studio has made before. But before that, Switch 2 owners have a chance to experience the studio's biggest hit for the first time thanks to a port of Elden Ring and its expansion, Shadow of the Erdtree. For the most part, the new v",
      "body": "FromSoftware is hoping to make a splash on the Switch 2 later this year when it launches The Duskbloods, a gothic competitive multiplayer game that’s unlike anything the studio has made before. But before that, Switch 2 owners have a chance to experience the studio’s biggest hit for the first time thanks to a port of Elden Ring and its expansion, Shadow of the Erdtree. For the most part, the new version — called the “Tarnished Edition” — is the same game that captivated players in 2022. But the shift in platform also makes it a particularly compelling way to explore the Lands Between.\nElden Ring differentiated itself from past FromSoft games by introducing a large open world shrouded in mystery and brimming with danger. And as we’ve learned over the years, open-world games work especially well on Nintendo’s hybrid console, letting you play in small bursts while in portable mode before settling in for grander adventures on your television. Nintendo made this point crystal clear launching the original Switch alongside The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild, and the extra horsepower of the Switch 2 has been put to use for ports of gigantic games like Cyberpunk 2077.\nIn many ways Elden Ring is an ideal test case for the Switch 2. It may be a few years old, but it’s a big and demanding game, one where every element matters. Wonky frame rates could mean death in battle, while dark or fuzzy graphics could allow a cave-dwelling creature to get the jump on you. I spent dozens of hours exploring and dying in the PS5 version of the game when it first launched, and I’ve since spent around 10 hours dabbling with the Switch 2 port. And while the Switch 2 edition isn’t the most technically impressive version of the game, it is one that runs just well enough to possibly be my favorite way to play.\nReplaying the opening hours of Elden Ring is an interesting experience. It’s easy to forget just how unforgiving the game can be early on. You play as a weak little wretch known as a Tarnished, thrust into an expansive fantasy realm full of things that want to kill you. One of the first things you see is a giant gladiator riding a horse, and if you try to attack it you’ll die from a single swipe of its sword. It’s a powerful learning experience, and much of my time with the Switch 2 version of the game has been spent cowering in bushes and slaying flocks of giant bats in order to slowly level up my fledgling astrologer. I’ve made some progress — I finally took down Margit, the Fell Omen, the game’s first major boss, and I made it to the Roundtable to get a comforting hug from Fia — but mostly I’ve just been puttering around, cautiously poking at the edges of the world, slowly growing in both strength and confidence.\nThe most important thing about my experience so far is that the limitations of the hardware never had a negative impact. There are some Switch 2 drawbacks, like less detailed characters and some textures that look muddy or washed out. But these are all superficial things, and I found myself largely forgetting about them the more that I explored. I haven’t come across any issues of stuttering or frame rate drops, either in portable or TV mode. Which is all to say that Elden Ring performs well enough on the Switch 2 that, aside from the occasional long load, I haven’t spent much time thinking about the game’s performance at all.\nInstead, I’ve found myself once again lost in the Lands Between, spending many of my waking moments reliving adventures or dreaming up where I want to go next. As with other big games on the Switch or its successor, the nature of the platform makes it easier to fit an adventure of this scale into my life. Sometimes, I grab the tablet and just spend 20 minutes clearing out a camp full of soldiers or seeking out the next Site of Grace, making incremental progress that will make life easier when I tuck in to explore for hours in TV mode.\nThis isn’t a new revelation about the Switch&nbsp;by any stretch. But with Elden Ring’s sprawling landscape and unforgiving combat, it’s an ideal example of the type for how well this kind of open world can work on the Switch 2. And with The Duskbloods lurking in the shadows, it’s a great way to scratch that FromSoft itch while waiting for the studio’s next game.\nElden Ring: Tarnished Edition launches on the Nintendo Switch 2 on August 28th.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Games Review",
        "Gaming",
        "Nintendo"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew Webster",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T14:00:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/08/assets_2026_06_1780581492_Torrent.png?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.4613147178592,100,93.077370564282",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/983307/elden-ring-nintendo-switch-2-review",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-26-meta-settles-for-18-billion-in-lawsuit-brought-by-29-states-",
      "title": "Meta settles for $18 billion in lawsuit brought by 29 states over social media harms to children",
      "summary": "The lawsuit alleged that Meta knowingly designed platforms like Instagram and Facebook to addict children, despite knowing about the harms the platforms could pose to young users.",
      "body": "Meta has agreed to pay up to $18 billion to settle claims from 29 U.S. states over children’s safety.\nThe lawsuit alleged that the social media giant knowingly designed platforms like Instagram and Facebook to addict children, despite knowing about the harms the platforms could pose to young users. The states also claimed that Meta knowingly collected data from children without parental knowledge, which violated the Children’s Online Privacy Protection Act (COPPA).\nBy settling, Meta is not admitting that it’s guilty of these claims, but it shows the company’s reluctance to move forward with a jury trial.\nMeta is framing its settlement as a call to action for YouTube and TikTok to adopt a set of protections for teens and controls for parents, which it developed in collaboration with 52 state attorneys general. Pending judicial approval, these protections will include the following terms, which will remain for 10 years:\nA default two-hour daily time limit, cumulative across both Facebook and Instagram; at 60 and 90 minutes, teens will be alerted about their daily usage, and they will also see prompts at 15-minute intervals to “encourage intentional use,” per Meta’s description\nA default block from Meta’s apps between midnight and 6 AM (known as “Night Mode”), plus muted notifications from 8 AM to 3 PM, when teens should be in school (“School Mode”)\nA default block for teens on seeing the number of likes and reactions on both their own posts and others’ posts\nA block for teens on using what Meta describes as “extreme makeup filters,” building on its existing block of cosmetic surgery filters\nFurther investment in age assurance technology to proactively identify accounts that might belong to users under 13 years old, or accounts that might belong to teens but were registered with an adult birthday\nWhile Meta already defaults teens into private Facebook and Instagram accounts, the company says it will strengthen its work to prevent adults from finding, following, and interacting with teens. Meta has also vowed to strengthen its existing parental controls and improve its response times to harmful content reports from teens.\nMeta adds that direct messages are not included in time limits or muted notification periods so that teens can “stay connected with friends and family.”\nMeta’s $18 billion settlement will be distributed to states over a 10-year period, but 30% of that settlement — about $5.3 billion — will only be paid out if YouTube and TikTok implement a one-hour daily time limit, Night Mode, and age-assurance measures. These platforms must also agree to pay an amount matching the 30% figure.\n“Because teens move fluidly across dozens of apps, we need an industry-wide solution,” Meta’s Chief Legal Officer C.J. Mahoney said in a statement. “We therefore call on our industry peers, TikTok and YouTube, to implement this new framework, right away.”\nMeta says that it will pay a $10 billion legal expense in the third quarter of this year, which will impact the company’s expenses and profit; however, Meta’s stock has risen on the news of the settlement.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nAmanda Silberling is a senior writer at TechCrunch covering the intersection of technology and culture. She has also written for publications like Polygon, MTV, the Kenyon Review, NPR, and Business Insider. She is the co-host of Wow If True, a podcast about internet culture, with science fiction author Isabel J. Kim. Prior to joining TechCrunch, she worked as a grassroots organizer, museum educator, and film festival coordinator. She holds a B.A. in English from the University of Pennsylvania and served as a Princeton in Asia Fellow in Laos.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Amanda by emailing amanda@techcrunch.com or via encrypted message at @amanda.100 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Amanda Silberling",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T13:43:01.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/zuckerberg-LA-trial-2026-GettyImages-2261841364.jpg?w=1024",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/26/meta-settles-for-18-billion-in-lawsuit-brought-by-29-states-over-social-media-harms-to-children/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-26-robot-brain-builders-are-pushing-out-of-their-gpt-2-era",
      "title": "Robot brain builders are pushing out of their GPT-2 era",
      "summary": "Physical AI is one of the hottest sectors in venture investing, with companies raising billions to apply the tools that gave large language models to robotics. That excitement helped deliver a big IPO for Unitree, China’s leading robot maker, which saw the company valued at $66 billion after its arrival on China’s equivalent of the Nasdaq. This week, however, the bottom fell ou…",
      "body": "Physical AI is one of the hottest sectors in venture investing, with companies raising billions to apply the tools that gave large language models to robotics.\nThat excitement helped deliver a big IPO for Unitree, China’s leading robot maker, which saw the company valued at $66 billion after its arrival on China’s equivalent of the Nasdaq. This week, however, the bottom fell out, and the company lost nearly half of its value. Analysts point to one obvious issue: While the robots’ physical capabilities are improving, they still lack the know-how to actually do value-creating work.\nAt last week’s Actuate conference, a gathering of developers building AI brains for robots, the excitement was clear. The event has tripled in size since it kicked off in 2023, and had 1,500 attendees, according to the organizer, Foxglove, a company that helps physical AI model builders manage and visualize their data.\nThe risk was also evident: A sign on a booth for Avala, another physical AI infrastructure player, promised to solve “the robotics data crisis.”\nThat crisis is the lack of high-quality training data for AI models. Attempts to build generalized robots that can do any task are still far off, and using end-to-end learning for specific tasks still hasn’t delivered products with reliable, commercial performance. For developers, the answer is to better mimic the advances of the frontier AI labs — find or create more diverse datasets, mess with different training regimes, and figure out better reinforcement learning scenarios.\nHarry Mellsop, a founder of Antioch, a startup building simulation tools for model builders, suggests physical AI is in its “GPT-2 era,” the OpenAI model that pre-dated the arrival of ChatGPT. More data and compute will be needed to get over the hump, particularly GPUs optimized for ray tracing, which are used to create high-fidelity simulations.\nThe furthest ahead are autonomous vehicles, in part because of the ability to collect relevant data from cars driven by people, and in part because the main task is to avoid contact, not manipulate the physical environment. Much of the tooling for model-building comes from autonomous vehicle companies; Foxglove, for example, was founded by former employees at Cruise, General Motor’s erstwhile self-driving effort.\nAnd now those car companies are increasingly betting that their investments in ML tooling will allow them to compete with dedicated humanoid makers. Tesla is already trying this with its Optimus robot, and now both AV-focused Wayve and rideshare giant Uber have launched robotics labs focused on humanoid form factors as R&D efforts.\n“I think you need to start in vehicles…manipulation robotics is like self-driving five years ago,” Alex Kendall, the CEO of Wayve, told TechCrunch. “The data infrastructure, the simulation, ML ops infrastructure, will probably be shared, but the specific world model for the simulator will be a different post-training. There’s going to be a lot more commonality than not, but then there’s going to need to be  some differences for different embodiments.”\nKendall argues that it’s too early to commit to any one hardware platform — advances in sensors and other components are coming quickly, and a truly general model should be more agnostic.\nThéophile Gervet, the CEO of Genesis AI, a vertically integrated humanoid robotics company that raised a $105 million seed round this year, disagreed, telling TechCrunch “we’re too early in this wave for a brain strategy to work; our take is there’s lots of opportunities to co-design hardware and AI.”\nGervet also touched on another hot topic in the sector: How specifically to focus your physical AI business. Robotics companies that are targeting specific tasks are getting their robots out in the field — Gritt is building solar farms, Agility is deploying robots in industrial settings, and Bedrock is operating excavators autonomously. Meanwhile, general-purpose humanoids aren’t getting out of the labs.\n“No customer cares about the general-purpose robot that works at 80% success rate,” Gervet said of the dilemma. “We see a lot of other players go general, but there is no value provided because there’s no vertical focus. … but then, if you’re building [for a narrow] vertical on top of GPT-2, you’re going to get crushed by the company building on GPT-4.”\nThe temptation to invest in a specific vertical, however, is tempting because it provides not just revenue but also real-world deployment data. While task-specific data might not have enough diversity to push general-purpose models forward, it is important for making a robot that adds value. Bedrock CTO Kevin Peterson noted that his company was just starting with excavation as a way to understand the challenges of “manipulation in the wild,” but plans to develop an intelligence layer that stretches across a series of construction machines.\nManaging all that data is a challenge, especially because of the density of visual and lidar data. Foxglove announced a new product this week, built on top of Nvidia’s Cosmos open-weight world model, which allows engineers to search that data with sophisticated natural language queries to build out evaluations and simulations. The goal is faster triage and debugging so model builders can iterate faster.\nSo what will be the fabled ChatGPT moment for physical AI that Sam Altman recently said is just a few years away? Kendall points out that the largest robot deployment in the world is still consumer vacuum bots. For him, a ChatGPT moment would be something that excites consumers, not investors, who seem to be plenty excited already.\n“One example of that would be when you get eyes-off autonomy for less than $1,000 [worth of hardware] in a car,” Kendall says; not coincidentally, his company is licensing models to car makers in an effort to produce just that. And that business, which he sees as a multibillion-dollar opportunity, will allow them to build a truly general embodied AI model.\nFor Gervet, the moment when physical AI becomes real is “manipulation that just works out of the box. You can talk to a robot in natural language and have it do any basic task for manipulation, like say pushing, pulling, closing a laptop, cleaning up a table, whatever you want to do, and it works to some level of reliability, let’s say 80% plus out of the box — that’s roughly your ChatGPT experience.”\nAdrian Macneil, Foxglove’s CEO, looks at the question a bit differently.\n“There will not be a ChatGPT moment for robotics,” he told TechCrunch. “The thing that made ChatGPT a moment in time was the distribution — they went from zero to like a million active users in like a week… distribution in the real world is way harder than that, right? I would be very excited for the Apple II moment in robotics or the IBM PC moment in robotics. When can I buy like a home robot that is gonna start doing some useful and fun stuff?”\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Tim Fernholz",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T13:30:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/unitree-robots.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/26/robot-brain-builders-are-pushing-out-of-their-gpt-2-era/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 6,
      "language": "en"
    },
