      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-26-bluesky-now-lets-you-upload-10-minute-long-videos",
      "title": "Bluesky now lets you upload 10-minute long videos",
      "summary": "Bluesky's new 10-minute video support includes faster upload speeds, too.",
      "body": "6:12 AM PDT · August 26, 2026\nImage Credits:Matteo Della Torre/NurPhoto (opens in a new window) / Getty Images\nSocial network Bluesky said on Tuesday that it is now increasing video limits on the platform to 10 minutes. Previously, the platform allowed three-minute videos, a limit that it raised last year. This makes it more competitive with other text-based platforms that by default allow for shorter video uploads.\nIn addition to increasing the video length, the company said in the post that users can now upload a maximum file size of 300MB. Plus, it mentioned that upload speed is now 2x-3x faster.\nThe new limit is on par with TikTok’s limit of 10-minute videos recorded directly with the app. The change also means that other apps running on the AT Protocol that underpins Bluesky, such as Bluescreen, Reelo, and Skylight, will also be able to serve longer videos\nSocial platforms have made concerted efforts to make their services video-friendly. Meta-owned rival Threads allows five-minute videos on its site.\nWhile X lets free users upload 140-second videos, it has made it easier for paying users to upload 3- to 4-hour videos with sizes up to 16GB to increase video consumption on its network. The company also launched a new video editor to encourage users to upload original content on its platform.\nWith this move, Bluesky is attempting to increase its active user base, which has dropped by over 25% year-over-year as of July, as per data from Similarweb.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Ivan Mehta",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T13:12:36.000Z",
