      "id": "bbc-2026-08-26-why-tesla-has-been-caught-up-in-a-massive-car-recall-in-chin",
      "title": "Why Tesla has been caught up in a massive car recall in China",
      "summary": "The country's biggest ever car recall affects more than four million vehicles made by Tesla, XPeng, Xiaomi and other firms.",
      "body": "Hidden door handles are widely used in modern cars in China, with around 60% of the top 100 best-selling new energy vehicles, including EVs and hybrids, having them.The minimalist design gives vehicles a \"sleek look and reduces wind drag\" but can be confusing to use, car industry analyst Stephen Dyer from consultancy AlixPartners told the BBC.\"Even I often find it difficult to locate the switch to open the door from the inside of an unfamiliar vehicle,\" said Dyer, who is based in Shanghai.But beyond their usability, the handles have been blamed for endangering the lives of drivers and passengers.Electronically powered door handles became an industry trend, but raised \"real safety concerns\" when doors failed to open promptly, delaying rescue efforts after accidents, said Nanyang Technological University associate professor Lyu Chen.\"A door handle may seem simple, but it becomes safety-critical in the seconds after a crash,\" Chen said.Chinese carmaker Xiaomi faced intense scrutiny following two fatal crashes in 2025 involving its SU7 model, when suspected electrical failures allegedly trapped occupants.One of the incidents took place on a highway in Tongling city, eastern China, killing three people, according to regional news coverage cited by state-controlled media Global Times.Relatives of the victims said the passengers were stuck in the car as the vehicle's doors could not be unlocked after the collision, the outlet said.At the time, Xiaomi's representatives said the SU7 had mechanical emergency release handles that function even without power, which are located at the bottom of each door.The company added at the time that it was investigating the matter and cooperating with the police.The latest models of the SU7 come with new door handles that can be opened without power. Xiaomi has not responded to BBC requests for comment.Tesla has also faced lawsuits in the US over its door handle designs.The company has been sued by the only survivor of a crash in 2024 involving a Cybertruck - Tesla's electric pickup - that killed three college students in California.Survivor Jordan Miller alleged that the Cybertruck's design trapped passengers in the burning vehicle and blocked their rescue.The car did not have door handles and the electronic buttons meant to open the doors failed to work, according to his lawyers from The Veen Firm.\"When you design a vehicle with no mechanical way to open the doors from the outside, you are betting the electronics will work in every scenario, including a high-speed crash followed by a fire,\" said one of the lawyers in a statement.In court filings, Tesla denied any wrongdoing and said the Cybertruck is compliant with US federal safety standards, the Guardian reported.There have been other cases in the US of passengers, including children, allegedly being trapped in Tesla cars after the doors suddenly stopped working.The BBC has contacted Tesla for comment.Some engineers say the measures may not go far enough to resolve safety concerns.Engineering instructor Lim Hong Wee said the measures do not address the need for a mechanical way to open the doors.Access to a car during an emergency should not depend on software, said Lim from the National University of Singapore.The measures mainly address the issue of people being able to get out of a vehicle, but do not provide a reliable way for rescuers to get in, said Gokul Krithivasan from risk management consultancy SecureSafe.Warning labels are also a \"weak remedy\", he added. \"When panicked, people fall back on their instinctive responses, and don't read small print in a dark and possibly smoke-filled cabin.\"For now, it is unclear whether there will be similar recalls outside China.The BBC understands that carmakers have not yet notified authorities of any plans for a similar recall in the US.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-26T03:33:59.000Z",
