      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-25-that-fake-grand-theft-auto-vi-demo-is-actually-just-malware",
      "title": "That fake Grand Theft Auto VI demo is actually just malware",
      "summary": "Grand Theft Auto fans, eager for news about one of the most anticipated video games of all time, appear especially vulnerable to this new cyberattack.",
      "body": "8:48 AM PDT · August 25, 2026\nImage Credits:Leon Neal / Getty Images\nIn the long wait for the highly anticipated Grand Theft Auto VI, an open-world game about crime, some in the gaming community are being targeted by cybercriminals in the real world.\nFans are still waiting for the game more than a decade after the release of GTA 5, which sold more copies than any game ever, except for Minecraft. Now, after multiple GTA 6 delays and with some fans desperate for any scrap of news, hackers are using the cover as a perfect opportunity to target eager gamers.\nCybercriminals have created fake websites impersonating Rockstar, the developer behind GTA 6. These websites advertise a playable GTA 6 demo — but no legitimate demo exists. Once the unsuspecting gamer clicks the “Play Now” button, they download a file that looks like a game installer, but is actually malware, according to the cybersecurity company Malwarebytes.\nThe malware is an information stealer that is designed to find and take a victim’s passwords, cookies, and logged-in sessions stored in their web browser. These attacks can in some cases allow hackers to log in as if they were the account holder, even when protected by multi-factor authentication.\nAfter multiple delays, GTA 6 is expected to release on November 19, 2026. On Thursday, Netflix will air an extended look at the game, which could perhaps answer some questions about the legitimacy of various leaks.\nA relevant reminder as always: Don’t download stuff if you aren’t sure it comes from a reliable source.\nThe fake Rockstar games website with a malware downloaderImage Credits:TechCrunch (screenshot)\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Amanda Silberling",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T15:48:38.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/grand-theft-auto-2213757731.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/25/that-fake-grand-theft-auto-vi-demo-is-actually-just-malware/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
