      "id": "techcrunch-2026-08-25-apple-rescues-hide-my-email-feature-from-the-privacy-scrap-h",
      "title": "Apple rescues Hide My Email feature from the privacy scrap heap",
      "summary": "Apple says it will no longer ditch using its icloud.com domain for hiding people's email addresses.",
      "body": "7:46 AM PDT · August 25, 2026\nImage Credits:Bryce Durbin / TechCrunch\nApple has reneged on a planned change to its Hide My Email feature that users said would have made the privacy tool less effective.\nHide My Email is a privacy feature for paying iCloud+ users that generates a unique and disposable email address to give websites instead of a person’s real email address and still have emails route to their regular inboxes. The benefit of the email-hiding feature is that privately generated email addresses cannot be distinguished from regular Apple users with an @icloud.com email address. However, the feature still allows Apple to tell law enforcement who is behind a masked email address.\nEarlier this year, the company said it would move users to @private.icloud.com, which users said would make it easier for websites to reject these email addresses during sign-ups. On social media and Reddit threads, users criticized the change as defeating the purpose of the service altogether.\nThe company confirmed an update on Monday that Hide My Email addresses “will remain” on @icloud.com, after previously saying it would move users to @private.icloud.com.\nApple did not give a reason for changing its decision, but Daring Fireball’s John Gruber, who first reported the about-face, said that Apple employees had objected to the change all along.\nEarlier this year, 404 Media reported that a bug in Hide My Email revealed people’s real email addresses. Apple fixed the bug more than a year after it was first made aware that emails rejected as spam would leak the masked users’ real email addresses.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Zack Whittaker",
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T14:46:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/icloud-pattern-with-key-red.jpg?resize=1200,675",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/25/apple-rescues-hide-my-email-feature-from-the-privacy-scrap-heap/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-25-us-judge-rejects-ghislaine-maxwell-s-frivolous-appeal-agains",
      "title": "US judge rejects Ghislaine Maxwell's 'frivolous' appeal against conviction",
      "summary": "The former Jeffrey Epstein accomplice loses her latest effort to be released from prison.",
      "body": "Ghislaine Maxwell's latest bid to overturn her sex trafficking conviction has been rejected by a US judge, who dismissed her claims as \"frivolous\" and \"meritless\". The 64-year-old is serving a 20-year prison sentence for her role in procuring girls for the late sex offender Jeffrey Epstein after being found guilty in December 2021.Maxwell had appealed against her five felony convictions, claiming her constitutional rights had been \"violated in multiple respects\".But a New York judge flatly rejected the appeal, describing it as \"lengthy and rambling\".Maxwell said Epstein-related documents released by the US Justice Department earlier this year supported her case that she had been wrongly convicted.But the judge ruled that \"far from exculpating her, it incriminates her or reinforces the correctness\" of her conviction.The judge also said her appeal \"falls very far from the mark\", adding: \"Nearly all its claims are procedurally barred; the overwhelming testimonial and documentary evidence adduced at trial conclusively established her guilt; and her petition's claims are demonstrably meritless, and generally based on speculation, distortions, and/or outright falsehoods. \"There is no fact that Maxwell fairly puts in dispute that has any potential to disturb the outcome of her trial.\"The judge warned Maxwell against making further appeals, saying it \"would not be taken in good faith\" by the court. Maxwell was found guilty in 2021 of facilitating Epstein's abuse. Prosecutors said she recruited and groomed girls, some as young as 14, between 1994 and 2004, before they were abused by Epstein, who died in prison in 2019.Maxwell appeared virtually before the US House Oversight Committee in February this year but refused to answer questions, invoking her right to remain silent as she pleaded the Fifth Amendment.Republican House Oversight Committee chairman James Comer said the situation was \"very disappointing\", adding: \"We had many questions to ask about the crimes she and Epstein committed, as well as questions about potential co-conspirators.\"Maxwell has previously sought a pardon from US President Donald Trump. When asked last October whether he would consider pardoning Maxwell, Trump said he \"wouldn't consider it or not consider it\", adding: \"I don't know anything about it, so I will speak to the [Department of Justice].\"The US Senate last month passed a resolution declaring its opposition to a presidential pardon for Maxwell.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T14:42:17.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/c294/live/fffcf3c0-a088-11f1-aed2-8d6da8d75094.jpg",
