    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-25-china-warns-it-will-safeguard-its-interests-after-us-widens-",
      "title": "China warns it will safeguard its interests after US widens sanctions against Iran",
      "summary": "The US has threatened to isolate nations that continue to do business with Tehran, which sells much of its oil to Beijing.",
      "body": "China has vowed to protect its interests after the US announced plans to widen economic sanctions against Iran and its trading partners, which include Beijing.Foreign ministry spokesman Lin Jian said China was firmly opposed to what it called \"illegal unilateral sanctions\" and would take \"all necessary measures\" to safeguard its rights.This came after US Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent announced the new measures on Monday, warning any nation financially partnering with Iran would be isolated.China is the biggest buyer of Iranian oil, although the trade has declined under the US blockade of Iran's ports.Lin said: \"Cooperation between China and Iran has always been conducted within the framework of international law and should not be interfered with or disrupted.\"Bessent described the plans for sanctions as \"the single greatest financial offensive ever\" against Iran, warning banks and businesses would share in Iran's isolation if they refused to cut ties with the country.He declined to focus on specific nations, but said US President Donald Trump would be phoning world leaders \"with specific requests to cease their interactions with the regime\".However, responding to a question about Chinese banks, Bessent said he wanted to make clear \"no one was above the reach of US sanctions\".The announcement comes ahead of planned talks between Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping next month. Washington is likely to be wary of potential retaliation from Beijing, which processes the majority of the world's rare earths and other critical minerals, crucial in the manufacture of many high-tech products.Beijing has already tightened export controls on rare earths as part of previous trade negotiations with the US.China's response followed Iranian Economy Minister Ali Madanizadeh saying Tehran was \"fully prepared\" for the wider sanctions, which he said would lead to \"another defeat\" for the US.\"The government is and was ready and has a two-year plan to manage these events,\" he told state television.\"We also have our own tools and know how to play the game,\" he said, adding Tehran had been \"waiting for these plans for a long time\".The US has dubbed its latest sanctions \"Operation Economic Outcast\" and described them as an \"economic D-Day\" against Iran.It comes almost six months after the start of the Iran war.The conflict has since led to hikes in oil prices worldwide, with Tehran effectively blocking exports through the Strait of Hormuz - a waterway vital to the global economy. The US has also slowed traffic via its own naval blockade.Recent attempts to end the conflict via diplomacy have failed, and a 60-day ceasefire formally expired last week with no sign of a settlement.With the announcement of new sanctions, the US treasury secretary claimed America was \"no longer managing the Iranian threat, we are ending it\".Iran is already under heavy sanctions from the US, but Bessent said these go further.He said the Treasury had mapped financial channels, facilitators and networks used by Iran to evade sanctions to trade oil, imposing sanctions on almost 60 entities, individuals and vessels.But analysts had cast doubt on the latest measures even before China's response. David Oxley, chief climate and commodities economist at Capital Economics, told the BBC the direct impact on Iran's energy revenues would be \"somewhat of a damp squib\". \"We suspect that the new package will have only a limited direct impact on Iranian energy flows in the short term,\" he added.This is partly due to the fact roughly 90% of Iran's oil goes to China, he said, \"which has not recognised US sanctions in the past and is unlikely to be cowed this time either\".Ali Vaez, deputy director of the Middle East and North Africa Program at the International Crisis Group, said: \"Generally, the Chinese are against unilateral sanctions. They would comply with multilateral or international sanctions, but unilateral sanctions just imposed by the US - they have always seen that as illegitimate.\"And although Iran's neighbours like Pakistan, Turkey and Iraq want to maintain good relationships with the US, they \"can't really afford to cut off ties with Iran\", Vaez told BBC Radio 4's Today programme.He said putting economic pressure on Tehran \"doesn't work\" because the Iranian regime is willing to \"absorb any pain\" and pass it onto the people.Other trade partners that could be affected by the US announcement include India and Russia, who have not yet responded.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T11:53:36.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/fc68/live/708dead0-a076-11f1-bf10-9547e884d7b7.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/clyl8965j5go?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-25-sir-billy-boston-the-world-class-athlete-whose-story-deserve",
      "title": "Sir Billy Boston - 'the world-class athlete, whose story deserved to be told'",
      "summary": "Rugby league commentator Matt Newsum says Sir Billy Boston was a \"world-class athlete, whose story deserved to be told\".",
      "body": "When Sir Billy Boston was knighted in 2025, it was a unique moment for rugby league.For the first time, this rebel sport from the north of England had been recognised in such a manner; one of its heroes had been acknowledged for their contribution to history through the game.Sir Billy was a fitting recipient of the honour and a world-class athlete, whose story deserved to be told.You can tell the importance of someone by how many statutes have been erected in tribute, and Sir Billy Boston wasn't lacking.His name was chosen as one of just five to grace Wembley's monument to rugby league's Challenge Cup, while years after his departure north, his home city of Cardiff also ensured his legacy remained cast-iron strong as he formed part of a three-man tribute along with Clive Sullivan and Gus Risman.Unsurprisingly, Boston is also captured in bronze in his adopted home of Wigan; the place where his legend really came to life.Born and brought up in Cardiff's ethnically-eclectic Tiger Bay district, he learned his rugby skills on the streets and with the talent factory-esque Cardiff International Athletic Club. Like all Welsh lads he harboured dreams of playing for his local club Cardiff RFC and hopefully the national team.Like others before and after for a time, Boston found those routes blocked, having to travel to Neath and Pontypridd to trial before his military service took him north.It was here where the league scouts came calling, and Wigan persuaded Boston to turn professional in 1953, forever ending his union dreams in the time of 'sine die' penalties for liaising with league personnel.The money was life changing. £3,000 could easily buy a house at that stage, while it proved money well spent as he took to the new code like a duck to water, scoring with his first touch in a Wigan A team game.This prime specimen of Welsh breezeblock had everything; he was quick, he could step and as former player and BBC commentator Ray French put it - his fend was like \"being hit in the face with shovel\".The sepia footage and slow shutter speeds of the cameras that captured his magic during his playing days simply cannot dull his brilliance. Whether it was bustling over for another try in cherry and white hoops at Wembley or crushing the Aussies at Sydney Cricket Ground, Boston was a blur of speed, footwork and power.Having swiftly progressed to the first team at Central Park, he would go on to score 478 tries in 488 games for Wigan, and tales of his feats would be passed down through generations. There are very few down at the Brick Community Stadium these days who don't know of Sir Billy, young or old, his name carried a cache right until his last.Great Britain gave him the international recognition denied to him by Wales, and he rewarded the selectors by racking up the tries, particularly against the Australians who would bear the brunt of his scoring ability.He scored 36 tries alone on his maiden Great Britain tour, dazzling both Australia and New Zealand in 1954 under captain and fellow Welshman Dickie Williams.Boston was a phenomenal rugby league player, but there is also a powerful message in how in post-war Britain a black athlete could become a hugely popular figure by communities adjusting to a new, more diverse world.Others like Roy Francis beforehand, Johnny Freeman, and Colin Dixon, also became established names, but never quite sprinkled the same stardust as Sir Billy.It paved the way for players such as Ellery Hanley and Martin Offiah to charm the public with their talents, while modern day talent such as Junior Nsemba and Kai Pearce-Paul continues to stand out in a sport which has broken down barriers for years.Sir Billy had a unique unifying effect on a normally partisan and tribal rugby league community. He was revered naturally in Wigan, his adopted home, but you won't hear a boo or jeer at mention of Boston anywhere - not even at sworn rivals St Helens where they appreciate a good footballer when they see one.Still a regular visitor to Wigan games right up to his passing, it was always a beautiful moment when the cameras found him, the crowd spotting him on the screen bursting into cheer, and the realisation creeping over his face in the shape of a smile.There was a photo of him and his wife Lady Joan, who had died just a few weeks previously, on her seat with flowers at Wigan's game with Wakefield last week. There is a poignancy that Sir Billy departed soon after her.His seat will always be reserved at Wigan, and he will be dearly missed but certainly not forgotten.",
