      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-it-s-not-alton-towers-rows-break-out-over-queuing-at-wales-h",
      "title": "'It's not Alton Towers' - rows break out over queuing at Wales' highest mountain",
      "summary": "There has been disagreement between queuers and others skipping the line to reach the top of Yr Wyddfa.",
      "body": "In May a pair of charity hikers were met with jeers from a queue at the summit as they tapped the trig point to complete a charity challenge.Jamie Richardson, 32, and Richard Thiedeman, 34, from Lincolnshire, were climbing Yr Wyddfa as part of the Three Peaks challenge to raise money for their friend who has motor neurone disease (MND).\"I was getting booed and then there was a gentleman who was at the top, who had just had his photo taken and he physically tried to stop me,\" Richardson said. He said he had just touched the summit point and heard someone say \"you should be ashamed of yourself\".Richardson added: \"There's no policing of it at all, it is purely free will, if you want to queue you can.\"The queuing, that is just purely down to British culture traditions.\"On social media, some described the scenes at the mountain over the May bank holiday weekend as having a carnival atmosphere due to the crowds there.A spokesman for the Eryri National Park Authority said it had \"no plans to intervene in a dispute over hikers queuing at the top of Yr Wyddfa\".He said: \"There are three quarters of a million people summitting annually, so queues are likely to be expected in peak times.\"He added there were no plans for new measures to be implemented this year, but the matter would be discussed at an end of visitor season meeting in the early autumn.",
