      "id": "theverge-2026-08-24-apple-s-four-pack-of-second-gen-airtags-is-20-off",
      "title": "Apple’s four-pack of second-gen AirTags is $20 off",
      "summary": "Apple’s four-pack of second-generation AirTags is down to $79 (originally $99) at Amazon and at Target, which is the bundle’s lowest price yet. You can get an AirTag for $24 right now piecemeal, but getting four together as a set is a way to save even more. Why pay $96 for four when you can save $17? It felt like we had to wait longer for the cost to fall this much for first-gen AirTags, so it’s a",
      "body": "Apple’s four-pack of second-generation AirTags is down to $79 (originally $99) at Amazon and at Target, which is the bundle’s lowest price yet. You can get an AirTag for $24 right now piecemeal, but getting four together as a set is a way to save even more. Why pay $96 for four when you can save $17? It felt like we had to wait longer for the cost to fall this much for first-gen AirTags, so it’s a little surprising to see the newer, better model available for less.\n[Product: Apple AirTag (second-gen) | https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GJTXVN9Z?tag=theverge02-20]\nThe second-generation AirTag looks identical to the first-generation AirTag. All of its upgrades are inside, and primarily include spec improvements to let you more easily find it. It includes boosted speaker volume, longer range, and overall better tracking capabilities. My colleague Victoria Song had a much easier time tracking the latest AirTag on different floors compared to the older model. Read our review.\nSome other great Verge-approved deals\n* The biggest Stream Deck that Elgato makes is down to its lowest price since Amazon’s Prime Day in late June. Originally $249.99 for the gadget with 32 programmable keys, it’s $50 off at Amazon and Best Buy, totaling $199.99. Each key can be customized within Elgato’s desktop software for Windows or macOS, letting you set not just the command that executes when you press it, but also the imagery that displays on the key. You can assign simple functions to keys, like muting a mic or turning off a webcam. The integrations run deep, though, letting you create multi-button macros that run with a single button press.\n* If you own a Switch 2 but still need a way to keep your original Switch’s Joy-Con recharged, there’s a great accessory that can charge Joy-Con for both systems and is half off at Amazon. Fastsnail’s charging dock that includes a USB-C-to-A cable and four Joy-Con charging sections is $6.99 (was already a great deal at $13.99) when you use the code NMSV9HX3, which the community at Slickdeals surfaced.\n* Backbone’s One controller is one of the best ways to play games on your phone. It offers a comfortable, complete set of controls, whether you’re playing native phone games, or streaming console or PC games. Normally about $100, the Backbone One PlayStation Edition that works seamlessly on iOS with Sony’s Remote Play app is just $27 in used condition at eBay. The seller VIPOUTLET claims that the product is in “fair/good condition” and may have some blemishes, and it includes a 60-day warranty. You can purchase a one-year warranty via Allstate on eBay for an extra $4. It’s a good price if you’re curious but have been turned off by its retail price.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Deals",
        "Gadgets",
        "Verge Shopping"
      ],
      "author": "Cameron Faulkner",
