      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-reform-says-it-will-prioritise-young-british-born-workers-fo",
      "title": "Reform says it will prioritise young British-born workers for social housing",
      "summary": "Labour called the plan \"unserious and unworkable\" and accused Reform of pushing \"gimmicks and division\".",
      "body": "Reform UK has said it will prioritise British-born workers under 35 when allocating social housing if it were to enter government.On top of British-born workers, former military service personnel and married couples with children will also be prioritised, Reform said.A Labour spokesperson called the plans \"unserious and unworkable\", saying it could leave British citizens without a home.Deputy leader Richard Tice has also set out plans to build 50,000 affordable social homes a year at a news conference, a policy which the party says will require no new taxes or borrowing.Tice said he wanted the rules on who was allocated a home to be changed away from need to suitability, putting British families, workers and veterans at the front of the queue, saying: \"This comes down to fairness.\"\"We're saying that the first people we should be looking after are young, British workers - under 35, young British married couples, young British veterans -  who have done the right thing,\" he said.\"They've got a job, they're doing their best, working in their community, setting a fine example, because the role of the British government is actually to look after British citizens first.\"Rules would be changed to reserve at least half of all social homes in England for British-born workers aged under 35, with prioritisation also given to married couples, veterans, victims of domestic violence and care leavers.Tice claimed there were currently 540,000 existing affordable social homes occupied by overseas nationals and he wanted that to change.\"It's not fair that young British working families can't get on the housing ladder when overseas nationals have come to the UK and, because of the current rules based on need rather than suitability, they have jumped ahead  in the queue,\" he said. \"We just don't think that's right: I can't think of another country where you rock up with a family and you expect the local taxpayer in that country to pay for your housing needs.\"UK or Irish nationals made up 88.5% of main social home householders in 2024/25, according to the English Housing Survey.That means around 11.5% of main householders, the per­son in whose name the social home is ren­ted, were non-UK or Irish nation­als.For its part, Reform estimates 540,000 social homes are currently occupied by foreign nationals.Reform has previously announced it would evict foreign nationals from social housing - a plan criticised by housing charity Shelter which said building more homes is the solution, rather than \"changing the order of the queue\" and \"stoking division\".Tice also said Reform would raise £10bn a year to build new homes, in part by reintroducing the Right to Buy scheme of the 1980s, allowing people to buy council homes at a discount. \"The failure then was they didn't reinvest the proceeds into building more affordable homes,\" he said, adding he believed reinvestment would raise a third of the investment needed to build 50,000 homes a year over a decade.Tice said another third would be raised via housing authorities, and the final third would be money from local government pension schemes.A Labour Party spokesperson said the plan was \"unworkable and unserious\", adding: \"It would blow a £10bn blackhole in the public finances, would create years of wrangling with the EU, and could leave British citizens without a place to call home.\"This Labour government is investing billions to deliver the biggest boost to social and affordable housing in a generation, to fix the housing crisis we inherited from the Tories.\"The Conservative Party, which also said it would revoke the right of foreign nationals to social housing, accused Reform of \"trying to catch up\" with its policies.The Tories have said their plan, which would allow Irish citizens and some EU nationals to remain in social housing, would free up around 230,000 homes.Sir James Cleverly, shadow housing secretary, added: \"The Conservatives have already set out comprehensive plans to ensure British nationals are at the front of the queue for social housing. Now Reform are trying to catch up.\"We also believe in a home owning society and that those who work hard should be able to buy their own home, without unnecessary barriers standing in their way.\"Liberal Democrat spokesperson Gideon Amos said that Reform's plan was a \"cruel gimmick\".Green MP Siân Berry said her party would tackle housing issues by ending Right to Buy, building and buying hundreds of thousands of new council homes, and introducing rent controls.She added: \"Another blatantly racist and cruel policy from Reform, adding to a host of plans to create a two-tier Britain – one for those born in the UK and one for everyone else.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T12:07:43.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/098c/live/b1e152d0-8819-11f1-981c-7d4dd1ef3d1d.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cvgjgyg1rngo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-london-zoo-animals-hop-on-scales-for-weigh-in",
      "title": "London Zoo animals hop on scales for weigh-in",
      "summary": "Kiwi the Capybara is among animals getting their annual health check while data is gathered and shared.",
      "body": "Image source, PA MediaImage caption, All animal statistics, including those of Kiwi the Capybara, will be shared with keepers across the globeAnimals at London Zoo are being checked and measured as part of its annual weigh-in day to monitor their health and wellbeing.Zookeepers will need to coax every bird, reptile, fish and  mammal - from the largest rodents in the world to the greenest katydids - to the weighing scale. Each measurement is added to the Zoological Information Management System (ZIMS), a global database shared with animal carers and conservationists that compares important information on thousands of endangered species.Many of the zoo's animals are trained to perch on a scale while zookeepers note down their measurements, but others will need to be tempted with a strategic snack.Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Katydids are famous for their leafy green camouflage and loud mating callsAlthough the zoo houses more than 14,000 animals, the keepers will only have time to measure and log the weight of about 400 creatures.Daniel Simmonds, London Zoo's animal operations manager, said: \"Regular weigh-ins help zookeepers track the health of our animals, including invertebrates like the magnolia land snails, the capybaras and Humboldt penguins, and genuinely help us manage the care of these animals, who play a vital role in our global conservation efforts.\"Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Humboldt penguins are listed as vulnerable in the wild due to threats such as climate change, overfishing and habitat disturbance.Among the animals weighed this year were capybara siblings Kiwi and Gizmo. Kiwi weighed in at 45kg (7st), while her brother Gizmo tipped the scales at 50.5kg (8st). While Giant Magnolia Land Snail No 2 weighed a modest 102.5g (3.6oz).Image source, PA MediaImage caption, Bolivian black-capped squirrel monkeys are native to Bolivia, Brazil and PeruImage source, PA MediaImage caption, The regular weigh-ins help zookeepers track the health of the zoo's creatures, including Vietnamese magnolia snailsRelated internet links",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T11:43:10.000Z",
