      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-fans-suffer-firework-burns-and-eye-injuries-in-eredivisie",
      "title": "Fans suffer firework burns and eye injuries in Eredivisie",
      "summary": "Dutch side SC Cambuur says fans were left with \"burns, hearing damage and eye injuries\"  after Feyenoord supporters threw fireworks during Sunday's Eredivisie match.",
      "body": "Feyenoord's general manager Robert Eenhoorn said he \"cannot be happy\" with their 5-2 victory in Leeuwarden, adding \"frustration prevails too much for that\".\"This problem obviously does not only affect Feyenoord and is unfortunately something we have been unable to put a stop to for decades,\" Eenhoorn said. \"We will therefore have to sit down again with all stakeholders, clubs, the KNVB [Dutch football association], and the authorities to look for a solution together.\"Leeuwarden mayor Foort van Oosten also condemned the incident and promised an investigation, while the Dutch football association said it would revisit its protocols for incidents of disorder.The game resumed despite the injuries to the fans, but it would have been abandoned had a player or match official been hit.\"That doesn't feel right,\" Jan Bluyssen, the KNVB's manager of professional football competition affairs, told Dutch news agency ANP.\"We need to examine whether the protocol is still appropriate and how we might want to adjust it.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T11:13:53.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/2955/live/4e45a290-9fa6-11f1-aed2-8d6da8d75094.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/articles/cr50n2d4rj0o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-dutch-broadcaster-avrotros-pulls-out-of-2027-eurovision-song",
      "title": "Dutch broadcaster Avrotros pulls out of  2027 Eurovision Song Contest",
      "summary": "It says contest can no longer be considered neutral while a participating country remains involved in a large-scale military conflict.",
      "body": "Dutch public broadcaster Avrotros has pulled out of next year's Eurovision Song Contest, saying that the competition can no longer be considered neutral while a participating country remains involved in a large-scale military conflict.In a statement, external released on Monday, the broadcaster said the decision was due to a lack of clarity from organisers - the Eurovision Broadcasting Union (EBU) - over assessing which countries could compete.Avrotros added: \"The severe humanitarian suffering in Gaza and the ongoing restrictions on press freedom have contributed to increasing divisions surrounding the Eurovision Song Contest in recent years.\"Earlier this month, the EBU updated its rules to state that if a country involved in an armed conflict wins the competition, it will be ineligible to host it the following year. But Avrotros said those changes \"do not provide sufficient confidence that ‌the ⁠independent and neutral character of the Eurovision Song Contest has been restored\".The broadcaster's withdrawal from next year's contest in Burgas, Bulgaria, does not necessarily rule out Dutch participation.Another public broadcaster could potentially take it on as the country has several.Avrotros pulled out of this year's event in Austria, Vienna, which the Netherlands also boycotted after Israel were allowed to compete.Only Ireland and Sweden have won Eurovision more than the Netherlands.Martin Green, director of the contest, said in a statement that the EBU regretted Avrotros's decision but respected its views. \"We will now continue our dialogue with NPO, the umbrella organisation for Dutch public service media, regarding the opportunities for another broadcaster to participate. We very much hope that audiences and artists in the Netherlands can continue to be represented in the Eurovision Song Contest.\"Taco Zimmerman, director general of Avrotos, said the Eurovision Song Contest \"was originally created to bring people and countries together\" but was now \"a platform for division\".  He said: \"It is undeniable that international conflicts are increasingly affecting the contest, undermining its neutral character.\"The Eurovision Song Contest has therefore become a platform for division. As a public broadcaster, we remain committed to our values.\"He added: \"They guide us in everything we do, even when that means stepping away from an event that is very close to our hearts. That is painful as we would have very much liked to be part of the contest again next year.\"Green, however, stated that the contest \"has always been a place to bring people together, celebrate diverse communities, and show that a better world is possible even in the most difficult times\".The NPO said it was \"very sorry\" to hear that one of its broadcasters had pulled out once again, adding \"we respect Avrotros's decision\".\"We also recognise that the geopolitical tensions are having a divisive effect,\" its response continued.\"That's why this hasn't been an easy decision for our colleagues. We will reflect on the upcoming edition and will come back to this at the right moment.\"The organisation concluded it was \"too early to comment on the next steps at this point\" and that its members were \"currently deliberating on that\".The recent amendment to the rules, that mean a country involved in an armed military conflict can't host the next contest if it wins, was approved by the contest's governing body, following feedback from participating broadcasters after Eurovision 2026.It stated that winning countries will also be disqualified from hosting if a \"sensitive geopolitical situation\" affects the \"security, safety or stability of their state or immediate region\".Last November, following a failed attempt to have Israel suspended, five broadcasters announced they were boycotting the competition - including Eurovision powerhouses Spain, Ireland and the Netherlands.Despite the protests, Israel's Noam Bettan ultimately took second place with his song Michelle.It marked the second year in a row that Israel had taken the runners-up position - and some participating broadcasters have privately expressed safety concerns over the contest being staged in Jerusalem or Tel Aviv, should the country win in the future.Although Israel will undoubtedly be seen as the target of the rule change, it will also affect Ukraine, which has been engaged in a protracted war since it was invaded by Russia in 2022.Ukrainian group Kalush Orchestra went on to win Eurovision that same year, in a symbolic show of public support.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T10:54:22.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/64e1/live/167c2180-9fca-11f1-8dfa-d5875b87c784.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c1kx3m2z332o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-russia-s-second-biggest-online-retailer-targeted-in-ukrainia",
      "title": "Russia's second-biggest online retailer targeted in Ukrainian strikes",
      "summary": "Ozon says its warehouses across southern Russia, including in Dagestan and Krasnodar, were hit in the overnight strikes.",
      "body": "Warehouses belonging to Russia's second-largest online retailer have been hit by a wave of Ukrainian drone strikes.Ozon said its logistics facilities in the southern regions of Krasnodar, Dagestan, Stavropol and Adygea were on fire and several people had been injured following overnight strikes.Ukraine has for weeks struck sites belonging to Wildberries, the country's biggest online retailer, describing the firm as a legitimate target because it supplied components to the military, which Moscow denies.Russian President Vladimir Putin said on Saturday that Ukraine had opened \"Pandora's box\" by striking economic targets and that Russia would hit back at Kyiv's \"most sensitive economic sectors\".Russia has targeted Ukraine's civilian infrastructure since launching its full-scale invasion in 2022, and recent Russian attacks have hit Ukrainian warehouses and cargo terminals.Both Russia and Ukraine deny deliberately targeting civilians.Ozon said \"a fire broke out\" at a logistics centre in Makhachkala, in the southern region of Dagestan, following a drone attack at around 05:00 local time (02:00 GMT) on Monday.The company said earlier that a \"major fire\" was burning at one of its logistics centres in the city of Krasnodar, 700km (435 miles) to the east of the Dagestan site.Russia's defence ministry said three children had been killed in Krasnodar following a Ukrainian drone attack. Russia's children's rights commissioner Maria Lvova-Belova said they were aged 13, 15 and 16, while six more children had been taken to hospital.Meanwhile, Ozon workers in Adygeysk, in the Adygea region, and Nevinnomyssk, in the Stavropol region, have been evacuated.Videos posted on social media of the Ozon attacks have been verified by the BBC. One shows a drone hitting a warehouse in the Orenburg region, while another shows a large plume of smoke and flames rising from its warehouse in Dagestan.Shares in Ozon fell by as much as 12% on the Moscow Exchange on Monday morning, while shares in its major shareholder, private equity firm AFK Sistema, fell by almost 13%.When asked on Monday whether the Russian government would provide state support to Ozon, the Kremlin said it was \"working with business representatives to develop various options for providing support in this difficult situation\".The overnight strikes comes a day after Ozon's site in the Samara region was hit. The company said a number of its workers had been injured.Following that strike, Ukraine's defence ministry said on Saturday: \"Systematic strikes on dual-use goods storage centres complicate the enemy's logistics and negatively affect the Russian economy as a whole.\"After Ukrainian units hit 15 of the largest logistics hubs of Wildberries, it was Ozon's turn.\"Meanwhile, in Ukraine's Odesa region, overnight strikes targeted food storage facilities and energy infrastructure leading to five people being injured, the region's head Oleg Kiper said on Telegram.Founded in 1998, Ozon is one of Russia's first online retailers. The company is second only to Wildberries in terms of scale and presence, with its pick-up posts and delivery boxes stationed across the country.Both the Ozon and Wildberries logos are often seen together in Russia at shared pick-up points. The targeting of these online retailers has had the effect of bringing the war closer to ordinary Russians.Both companies have been described as \"Russia's Amazon\" and it is estimated that they are used by 85-90% of Russia's economically active population. In many remote places lacking accessible shops, Russians depend on them for much of their clothes and household appliances. But unlike Amazon, which sells goods directly alongside its marketplace business, Wildberries and Ozon act almost entirely as marketplaces, connecting independent sellers with buyers across Russia, storing and delivering their goods and handling payments.This means many of the losses incurred in the attacks have been borne by those businesses.The Kremlin has denied that online platforms are used for army supplies.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T10:40:22.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/f16b/live/039a8ff0-9fa4-11f1-bff6-57f1422ebb0b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cg598yvrzpro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-08-24-dechambeau-proud-of-what-liv-has-achieved",
      "title": "DeChambeau proud of what LIV has achieved",
      "summary": "Bryson DeChambeau says he is \"proud\" of what LIV Golf has achieved with the breakaway league facing questions about its future following the final event of the season in Indianapolis.",
      "body": "Bryson DeChambeau says he is \"proud\" of what LIV Golf has achieved as the breakaway league faces questions about its future following the final event of the season in Indianapolis.The league, which launched in 2022 as an alternative to the PGA Tour, has faced an uncertain future since Saudi Arabia's Public Investment Fund (PIF) withdrew its financial backing in April.Last week, its season-ending Team Championships in Michigan was cancelled, meaning the event in Indianapolis concluded the 2026 campaign.LIV Golf CEO Scott O'Neil has said new investors have been secured for next season but has been tight-lipped on the league's future plans and structure.American DeChambeau, who led a selection of star players in joining LIV Golf four years ago, said although the breakaway league has not been perfect, it has \"a lot of potential moving forward\".\"It has been an interesting ride, that's for sure, everything we've gone through, and the tough moments we've had. It's made us stronger,\" said DeChambeau.\"As much as we are transitioning from LIV 1.0 to LIV 2.0, I have great thoughts about what could happen next year. I wish I could be more upfront about it. \"Ultimately I'm proud of the fact of what we've done, the good and some of the bad that we've unfortunately had, things that have occurred, I'm going to be real with it, not everything was perfect. \"But for the most part we've done a solid job and if there's one thing I could say, we did something different in the game of golf and I'm proud of that.\"At the end of the interview, DeChambeau added: \"I think there's something fun coming.\"Last week, Rory McIlroy said it could be a \"tough road back\" to the PGA Tour for those who leave LIV Golf.LIV spent £3.7bn, luring players like DeChambeau with lucrative contracts and significant prize money.Spain's Jon Rahm, who has been crowned the league's individual champion for a third consecutive year, has faced speculation about his future with LIV but says it has not affected his performances.\"There's been a lot that has surrounded all of us pretty much since we joined. At the end of the day, once you step on the golf course it's just golf,\" said Rahm.\"All the problems and theories go away and you compete. It hasn't been any harder or easier. That is the life of a pro athlete, you deal with things like that.\"American rookie Michael La Sasso pipped Rahm to win the event in Indianapolis, carding a final-round 65 to finish on 18-under-par.Rahm finished one shot behind on 17-under, with DeChambeau and Spain's Sergio Garcia tying for third a further shot back.La Sasso, 22, executed a perfect chip on the final hole before a tap-in birdie to become the youngest winner of a LIV Golf event.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-24T10:20:10.000Z",
