      "id": "bbc-2026-08-21-stormy-autumn-cold-winter-el-ni-o-could-bring-a-tough-few-mo",
      "title": "Stormy autumn, cold winter - El Niño could bring a tough few months for the UK",
      "summary": "This year's El Niño is expected to be the strongest in living memory and could lead to a range of impacts of the UK - including a stormy autumn.",
      "body": "Stormy autumn, cold winter - how might El Niño affect the UK weather?Image caption, Whilst no two events are the same, the current developing El Niño could mean a greater chance of stormy autumn weather in the UK. File image of Storm Herminia which hit the UK in January 2025ByBen RichLead Weather PresenterEl Niño is developing right now across the Pacific Ocean - thousands of miles away from the UK.Its most profound impacts may well be centred around this area, with the increased risk of flooding and landslides in Peru and Ecuador and the threat of droughts and wildfires in Australia.However the disruption caused to global wind patterns means the impacts will ripple right across the globe.And the Met Office says the UK weather is likely to be affected, with the growing chance that autumn and early winter will be wet and stormy.No two El Niños are the same - so, while it may increase the chance of certain types of weather occurring, this is not guaranteed.Storms likely by November\"I have not seen an event like this developing in our forecasts before\", said Professor Adam Scaife, head of long-range forecasts at the Met Office.He says the scale of the growing El Niño is unprecedented in modern climate records.As it approaches its peak by the end of 2026, its influence around the world will grow with effects spreading across the globe.Professor Scaife says forecasters \"already see signals for increased rainfall and storminess in autumn here in the UK\", especially into November and December.After a summer that looks set to be the UK's hottest on record and with the drought conditions that continue to grip large swathes of the country, a wet autumn sounds like good news.However, we know that climate change is making increasingly intense rainfall more likely, and so these storms and downpours will also bring the threat of flooding.Strong winds could also cause damage, transport disruption and power cuts.The winter of 2015 and 2016, which coincided with a very strong El Niño, brought a succession of major storms delivering what experts at the time described as the most extreme flooding on record.Figure caption, Watch: How does El Niño affect world weather?Could it be a cold winter?El Niño can be associated with cold spells in the late winter - but the link is not a strong one and the past evidence is mixed.The so-called Big Freeze of winter 2009/2010 was, at the time, the coldest in three decades - and this coincided with an El Niño.However, 2006/2007 was also an El Niño winter and that was unseasonably warm.The further forecasters look into the future the more uncertain the prospects become.So while El Niño may increase the chance of cold weather early in 2027, this is not guaranteed.Image source, ReutersImage caption, Storm Floris brought strong winds to parts of the UK in 2025, with gusts of up to 80mphWhat about next summer?Globally, a very strong El Niño typically lifts average temperatures by 0.2C.This may not sound like much, but on top of the heating effect of human-induced climate change it will increase the risks of heatwaves around the world.However, El Niño's effects are not the same in each individual place, and the links to hot weather in the UK are not clear.\"Long-term global warming remains the more significant driver of the UK's rising temperatures and increasing risk of extreme heat\" said Dr Akshay Deoras, a climate scientist at the University of Reading. \"It is still too early to say what next summer will bring for the UK\", added Dr Deoras, \"however El Niño's additional warming, coupled with the UK's already warmer climate, could provide the conditions for a hot summer.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-08-21T14:17:03.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_weather/1200/cpsprodpb/38f5/live/f8c01c40-9d45-11f1-a137-179a8d1c790b.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/weather/articles/cd0x17r4j0yo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
